import GroupApproximation.Sofic.SmallCancellationRouter
import GroupApproximation.Sofic.PeriodicOverlap
import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FinitelyPresentedGroup

/-!
# The bespoke router construction over the two-generator free group

`Sofic.SmallCancellationRouter` freezes the *output* of the routing lemma as
`RoutingLemmaData E N s B`: a common quotient of a source `E` and a Kazhdan
partner `B` in which the designated normal subgroup `N` fills everything and a
protected element `s` survives.  This module builds the *input* side over an
arbitrary relator family, so that an explicit word design has a typed target.

## The avatar architecture

The routed quotient is **not** a quotient of a free product.  It is

    Routed := FreeGroup (Fin 2) ⧸ ⟪relators⟫,

two-generated by construction, and the two maps into it are *defined by their
values on generators*, not induced by inclusions of factors:

* `route : E →* Routed` sends the `i`-th generator of a chosen presentation of
  `E` to a target word `W i` in the two free generators.  It is well defined
  because the relator family contains, for every relator of that presentation,
  its `W`-rewrite (`srcRel_rewrite`), so the presentation's kernel dies —
  `srcHom_ker_le_avatar_ker`, and the descent along the surjection
  `srcHom` is `descend`.
* `partner : B →* Routed` is the same construction with target words `U`.

This is what makes a *proper* common quotient possible.  The free-product
framing cannot: over `E ∗ B` a metric `C'(1/6)` family keeps every factor
embedded (Lyndon--Schupp; in Lean,
`OsinSmallCancellation.factorMap_injective_of_cyclicallyReduced`), while
`defect_top` forces the source factor to surject, so the routing map would have
to be an isomorphism and the partner could only be routed onto the source
itself.  That obstruction is recorded, proved, in
`Sofic.FreeProductRouterObstruction`; this module is the architecture that
avoids it.  Over a free group there is no factor to embed, and the small
cancellation hypotheses are exactly the vocabulary
`SmallCancellationRouter` already carries — `symmetrization`, `IsPiece`,
`MetricSmallCancellation`, `GreendlingerGate` — with no `Monoid.CoprodI`
anywhere.

## Saturation and two-generation

The other half of the design is the **tying relators**.  `tieDefect` asks that
each free generator be, modulo the family, a word in the avatar images of the
two designated elements of the defect `N`; that makes the two free generators
lie in `N.map route`, and since they generate the quotient it gives
`defect_top` — and `route_surjective` follows from `defect_top`, because the
image of a subgroup is inside the range.  `tiePartner` asks the same with words
in the partner's target words `U`, and gives `partner_surjective`.  Neither is
a conclusion in disguise: both are statements that a specific short word lies
in the normal closure of the family, which is what putting that word into the
family discharges.

## What is assumed, and where

`RouterConclusions` — injectivity of the presentation map on the protected
pair, and torsion-freeness of the routed quotient — is taken as an explicit
hypothesis by `torsionFree`, `protected_injOn` and `routerData`, and by nothing
else.  It is exactly the conclusion of
`GreendlingerFreeGate.router_conclusions_of_metric` at this design's data, and
`Sofic.BespokeRouterGateConsumption` discharges it from that theorem in one
line; the discharge lives in that separate module, and not here, because the
gate file still carries the two open classical leaves and **this module is
root-imported, so its whole transitive closure has to stay clean**.  Note that
nothing warns you at the call site: a declaration built from an open leaf
inherits its footprint silently, and the axiom probe in `scripts/Audit.lean` is
the gate of record.

The hypothesis is stated in the shape that theorem produces, so the two are
interchangeable without an adapter, and the design's own fields
(`relators_cyclicallyReduced`, `metric`, `relators_noProperPower`,
`protectedBall`) are precisely its hypothesis list.

The no-proper-power condition travelling with the metric condition is not
decoration: the `C'(1/6)` family generated by `(ab)^7` has no pieces at all,
while the image of `ab` in its quotient has order seven.  It is stated in the
word-level form — the one checkable by inspection of the finitely many relators
a design writes down — and unfolds to `GreendlingerFreeGate.NoProperPower`, so
it feeds that theorem directly.

Over the free-group alphabet `C'(1/6)` is the honest constant: there are no
syllable fragments, so the fragment correction that the free-product form needs
(`GreendlingerRelativeTransfer.FragmentSlack`, which forces the constant
strictly below `1/6`) does not apply here.

Everything else — `defect_top`, `generates`, `route_surjective`,
`partner_surjective`, finite presentation, and the well-definedness of both
maps — is derived unconditionally, with no appeal to the gate at all.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace BespokeRouter

open SmallCancellationRouter

/-! ## 0.  Two general lemmas -/

/-- Injectivity on `{1, s}` is exactly the statement that `s` is not killed. -/
theorem injOn_pair {G H : Type} [Group G] [Group H] {f : G →* H} {s : G}
    (h : f s = 1 → s = 1) : Set.InjOn f ({1, s} : Set G) := by
  intro x hx y hy hxy
  simp only [Set.mem_insert_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hx hy
  rcases hx with rfl | rfl
  · rcases hy with rfl | rfl
    · rfl
    · exact (h (by rw [← hxy, map_one])).symm
  · rcases hy with rfl | rfl
    · exact h (by rw [hxy, map_one])
    · rfl

section Descend

variable {F G H : Type} [Group F] [Group G] [Group H]

/-- **Descent along a surjection.**  A homomorphism that kills the kernel of a
surjection factors through it.  This is what makes "define the map on the
generators of a presentation" a construction rather than a wish: `f` is the
presentation map of the source, `θ` is the assignment of target words, and the
hypothesis is that every relation of the source holds among the target
words. -/
noncomputable def descend (f : F →* G) (hf : Function.Surjective f) (θ : F →* H)
    (hker : f.ker ≤ θ.ker) : G →* H :=
  (QuotientGroup.lift f.ker θ hker).comp
    (QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective f hf).symm.toMonoidHom

/-- The descended map computes the assignment on the presentation. -/
theorem descend_apply (f : F →* G) (hf : Function.Surjective f) (θ : F →* H)
    (hker : f.ker ≤ θ.ker) (x : F) : descend f hf θ hker (f x) = θ x := by
  have h1 : QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective f hf
      (QuotientGroup.mk x : F ⧸ f.ker) = f x := rfl
  have h2 : (QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective f hf).symm (f x)
      = (QuotientGroup.mk x : F ⧸ f.ker) := by
    rw [← h1, MulEquiv.symm_apply_apply]
  show QuotientGroup.lift f.ker θ hker
      ((QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective f hf).symm (f x)) = θ x
  rw [h2]
  rfl

end Descend

/-! ## 1.  The avatar: the two-generator free group and its quotient -/

/-- The router's avatar group: the free group on two generators.  The routed
quotient is two-generated because this one is. -/
abbrev RouterFree : Type := FreeGroup (Fin 2)

/-- The normal subgroup a relator family presents. -/
def relSubgroup (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) : Subgroup RouterFree :=
  Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R)

/-- Registered because instance search stops at the `def`, and the quotient
needs the instance to be a group. -/
instance relSubgroup_normal (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) :
    (relSubgroup R).Normal :=
  Subgroup.normalClosure_normal

/-- **The routed quotient.** -/
abbrev RouterQuotient (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) : Type :=
  RouterFree ⧸ relSubgroup R

/-- The presentation map onto the routed quotient. -/
def mkR (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) : RouterFree →* RouterQuotient R :=
  QuotientGroup.mk' (relSubgroup R)

/-- The two produced generators of the routed quotient. -/
def y (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) (i : Fin 2) : RouterQuotient R :=
  mkR R (FreeGroup.of i)

theorem mem_relSubgroup {R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))}
    {r : List (Fin 2 × Bool)} (hr : r ∈ R) : FreeGroup.mk r ∈ relSubgroup R :=
  Subgroup.subset_normalClosure ⟨r, hr, rfl⟩

/-- A relator of the shape `g · w⁻¹` identifies `g` with `w` in the quotient.
This is the only way the tying fields are ever used. -/
theorem mkR_eq_of_mem {R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))} {g w : RouterFree}
    (h : g * w⁻¹ ∈ relSubgroup R) : mkR R g = mkR R w := by
  have h1 : mkR R (g * w⁻¹) = 1 := (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).mpr h
  rw [map_mul, map_inv, mul_inv_eq_one] at h1
  exact h1

/-- **The routed quotient is two-generated**, unconditionally: the free group
is generated by its two generators and the presentation map is onto. -/
theorem closure_y_eq_top (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) :
    Subgroup.closure ({y R 0, y R 1} : Set (RouterQuotient R)) = ⊤ := by
  have key : ∀ g : RouterFree,
      mkR R g ∈ Subgroup.closure ({y R 0, y R 1} : Set (RouterQuotient R)) := by
    intro g
    induction g using FreeGroup.induction_on with
    | C1 =>
        rw [map_one]
        exact one_mem _
    | of i =>
        refine Subgroup.subset_closure ?_
        fin_cases i
        · exact Set.mem_insert _ _
        · exact Set.mem_insert_of_mem _ rfl
    | inv_of i ih =>
        rw [map_inv]
        exact inv_mem ih
    | mul x z hx hz =>
        rw [map_mul]
        exact mul_mem hx hz
  rw [eq_top_iff]
  intro q _
  obtain ⟨g, rfl⟩ := QuotientGroup.mk'_surjective (relSubgroup R) q
  exact key g

/-- **Finite presentation, unconditionally.**  Finitely many relators over the
free group on two generators: Mathlib's free-group instance, then its quotient
lemma. -/
theorem routerQuotient_finitelyPresented {R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))}
    (hR : R.Finite) : Group.IsFinitelyPresented (RouterQuotient R) :=
  Group.IsFinitelyPresented.quotient (relSubgroup R)
    ⟨FreeGroup.mk '' R, hR.image _, rfl⟩

/-- **No relator is a proper power**, at the level of words: no relator is some
shorter word written down `m ≥ 2` times.  This is
`GreendlingerFreeGate.NoProperPower` restated at the router's alphabet, and it
unfolds to the same thing, so a caller can feed it to that file's theorems with
no adapter.  It is restated rather than imported because this module has to
stay importable from the root while the gate file still has open leaves. -/
def NoProperPower (R : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))) : Prop :=
  ∀ r ∈ R, ¬ PeriodicOverlap.IsProperPower r

/-! ## 2.  The design -/

/-- **The word-design obligations.**  Everything an explicit relator family and
its target-word assignment have to satisfy, and nothing that is a conclusion of
the routing lemma: the family `relators` is understood to be symmetrized, the
convention `SmallCancellationRouter.IsPiece` is stated for. -/
structure RouterRelatorDesign (E : Type) [Group E] (N : Subgroup E) (s : E)
    (B : Type) [Group B] where
  /-- The symmetrized relator family, as reduced letter lists. -/
  relators : Set (List (Fin 2 × Bool))
  /-- Finitely many relators — the finite-presentation obligation. -/
  relators_finite : relators.Finite
  /-- Every relator is cyclically reduced. -/
  relators_cyclicallyReduced :
    ∀ r ∈ relators, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r
  /-- No relator is a proper power — the torsion clause's hypothesis, in the
  word-level form, which is the one checkable by inspection of the finitely
  many relators. -/
  relators_noProperPower : NoProperPower relators
  /-- The metric small-cancellation condition. -/
  metric : MetricSmallCancellation relators (1 / 6)
  /-- The number of generators of the chosen presentation of the source. -/
  srcCard : ℕ
  /-- The presentation map of the source. -/
  srcHom : FreeGroup (Fin srcCard) →* E
  /-- It is onto: the chosen generators generate. -/
  srcHom_surjective : Function.Surjective srcHom
  /-- A defining set of relators for that presentation. -/
  srcRel : Set (FreeGroup (Fin srcCard))
  /-- It is defining: every relation of the source follows from `srcRel`. -/
  srcHom_ker_le : srcHom.ker ≤ Subgroup.normalClosure srcRel
  /-- The target word of each source generator. -/
  W : Fin srcCard → RouterFree
  /-- **Source well-definedness**: the `W`-rewrite of every source relator dies
  in the routed quotient — in practice because it is one of the relators. -/
  srcRel_rewrite : ∀ r ∈ srcRel, FreeGroup.lift W r ∈ relSubgroup relators
  /-- The number of generators of the chosen presentation of the partner. -/
  parCard : ℕ
  /-- The presentation map of the partner. -/
  parHom : FreeGroup (Fin parCard) →* B
  /-- It is onto. -/
  parHom_surjective : Function.Surjective parHom
  /-- A defining set of relators for the partner's presentation. -/
  parRel : Set (FreeGroup (Fin parCard))
  /-- It is defining. -/
  parHom_ker_le : parHom.ker ≤ Subgroup.normalClosure parRel
  /-- The target word of each partner generator. -/
  U : Fin parCard → RouterFree
  /-- **Partner well-definedness**: the `U`-rewrite of every partner relator
  dies in the routed quotient. -/
  parRel_rewrite : ∀ r ∈ parRel, FreeGroup.lift U r ∈ relSubgroup relators
  /-- The first designated defect element, as a word in the source's
  generators. -/
  basisOne : FreeGroup (Fin srcCard)
  /-- The second designated defect element. -/
  basisTwo : FreeGroup (Fin srcCard)
  /-- The first designated element lies in the designated normal subgroup. -/
  basisOne_mem : srcHom basisOne ∈ N
  /-- The second designated element lies in the designated normal subgroup. -/
  basisTwo_mem : srcHom basisTwo ∈ N
  /-- **The defect tying relators**: each free generator is, modulo the family,
  a word in the avatar images of the two designated defect elements. -/
  tieDefect : ∀ i : Fin 2, ∃ v ∈ Subgroup.closure
      ({FreeGroup.lift W basisOne, FreeGroup.lift W basisTwo} : Set RouterFree),
    FreeGroup.of i * v⁻¹ ∈ relSubgroup relators
  /-- **The partner tying relators**: each free generator is, modulo the
  family, a word in the partner's target words. -/
  tiePartner : ∀ i : Fin 2, ∃ v ∈ Subgroup.closure (Set.range U),
    FreeGroup.of i * v⁻¹ ∈ relSubgroup relators
  /-- The protected element, as a word in the source's generators. -/
  protectedWord : FreeGroup (Fin srcCard)
  /-- It is a word for the protected element. -/
  protectedWord_spec : srcHom protectedWord = s
  /-- Its target word is nontrivial in the free group — the design's own half
  of protected survival. -/
  protectedWord_ne_one : FreeGroup.lift W protectedWord ≠ 1
  /-- **The protected ball condition**: every symmetrized relator is longer
  than twice the protected target word.  This is exactly the shortness
  hypothesis `injOn_mk'_of_greendlinger` consumes. -/
  protectedBall : ∀ r ∈ symmetrization relators,
    2 * FreeGroup.norm (FreeGroup.lift W protectedWord) ≤ r.length

namespace RouterRelatorDesign

variable {E : Type} [Group E] {N : Subgroup E} {s : E} {B : Type} [Group B]
    (D : RouterRelatorDesign E N s B)

/-- The routed quotient of a design. -/
abbrev Routed : Type := RouterQuotient D.relators

/-! ### The two maps, constructed -/

/-- The source's target-word assignment, as a homomorphism out of the free
group on the source's generators. -/
def srcAvatar : FreeGroup (Fin D.srcCard) →* D.Routed :=
  (mkR D.relators).comp (FreeGroup.lift D.W)

/-- The partner's target-word assignment. -/
def parAvatar : FreeGroup (Fin D.parCard) →* D.Routed :=
  (mkR D.relators).comp (FreeGroup.lift D.U)

/-- **Source well-definedness.**  Every relation of the source holds among the
target words, so the assignment descends. -/
theorem srcHom_ker_le_avatar_ker : D.srcHom.ker ≤ D.srcAvatar.ker := by
  refine le_trans D.srcHom_ker_le (Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal ?_)
  intro x hx
  exact MonoidHom.mem_ker.mpr
    ((QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).mpr (D.srcRel_rewrite x hx))

/-- **Partner well-definedness.** -/
theorem parHom_ker_le_avatar_ker : D.parHom.ker ≤ D.parAvatar.ker := by
  refine le_trans D.parHom_ker_le (Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal ?_)
  intro x hx
  exact MonoidHom.mem_ker.mpr
    ((QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).mpr (D.parRel_rewrite x hx))

/-- **The routing map**, defined by its values on the source's generators. -/
noncomputable def route : E →* D.Routed :=
  descend D.srcHom D.srcHom_surjective D.srcAvatar D.srcHom_ker_le_avatar_ker

/-- **The partner map**, defined by its values on the partner's generators. -/
noncomputable def partner : B →* D.Routed :=
  descend D.parHom D.parHom_surjective D.parAvatar D.parHom_ker_le_avatar_ker

theorem route_srcHom (x : FreeGroup (Fin D.srcCard)) :
    D.route (D.srcHom x) = mkR D.relators (FreeGroup.lift D.W x) :=
  descend_apply _ _ _ _ x

theorem partner_parHom (x : FreeGroup (Fin D.parCard)) :
    D.partner (D.parHom x) = mkR D.relators (FreeGroup.lift D.U x) :=
  descend_apply _ _ _ _ x

/-! ### The interface's fields, derived -/

/-- The first produced generator of the routed quotient. -/
def genOne : D.Routed := y D.relators 0

/-- The second produced generator of the routed quotient. -/
def genTwo : D.Routed := y D.relators 1

/-- **Two-generation, derived** — and unconditional: the avatar is free on two
generators. -/
theorem generates :
    Subgroup.closure ({D.genOne, D.genTwo} : Set D.Routed) = ⊤ :=
  closure_y_eq_top D.relators

/-- Words in the two designated defect elements land in the defect's image. -/
theorem mkR_mem_map_route {v : RouterFree}
    (hv : v ∈ Subgroup.closure
      ({FreeGroup.lift D.W D.basisOne, FreeGroup.lift D.W D.basisTwo} :
        Set RouterFree)) :
    mkR D.relators v ∈ N.map D.route := by
  have h : v ∈ (N.map D.route).comap (mkR D.relators) := by
    refine (Subgroup.closure_le _).mpr ?_ hv
    rintro x (rfl | rfl)
    · exact ⟨D.srcHom D.basisOne, D.basisOne_mem, D.route_srcHom D.basisOne⟩
    · exact ⟨D.srcHom D.basisTwo, D.basisTwo_mem, D.route_srcHom D.basisTwo⟩
  exact h

/-- Each free generator lies in the image of the designated normal subgroup:
the defect tying relator, unwound. -/
theorem y_mem_map_route (i : Fin 2) : y D.relators i ∈ N.map D.route := by
  obtain ⟨v, hv, hrel⟩ := D.tieDefect i
  rw [show y D.relators i = mkR D.relators v from mkR_eq_of_mem hrel]
  exact D.mkR_mem_map_route hv

/-- **Saturation, derived.**  The designated normal subgroup fills the routed
quotient.  This is the field the interface calls `defect_top`. -/
theorem defect_top : N.map D.route = ⊤ := by
  rw [eq_top_iff, ← D.generates, Subgroup.closure_le]
  rintro q (rfl | rfl)
  · exact D.y_mem_map_route 0
  · exact D.y_mem_map_route 1

/-- **The routing map is onto, derived** — from saturation alone, since the
image of a subgroup is inside the range. -/
theorem route_surjective : Function.Surjective D.route := by
  intro q
  have hq : q ∈ N.map D.route := by
    rw [D.defect_top]
    exact Subgroup.mem_top q
  obtain ⟨a, -, ha⟩ := hq
  exact ⟨a, ha⟩

/-- Each free generator lies in the partner's image: the partner tying relator,
unwound. -/
theorem y_mem_range_partner (i : Fin 2) : y D.relators i ∈ D.partner.range := by
  obtain ⟨v, hv, hrel⟩ := D.tiePartner i
  rw [show y D.relators i = mkR D.relators v from mkR_eq_of_mem hrel]
  have h : v ∈ (D.partner.range).comap (mkR D.relators) := by
    refine (Subgroup.closure_le _).mpr ?_ hv
    rintro _ ⟨k, rfl⟩
    refine ⟨D.parHom (FreeGroup.of k), ?_⟩
    rw [D.partner_parHom, FreeGroup.lift_apply_of]
  exact h

/-- **The partner map is onto, derived.** -/
theorem partner_surjective : Function.Surjective D.partner := by
  rw [← MonoidHom.range_eq_top, eq_top_iff, ← D.generates, Subgroup.closure_le]
  rintro q (rfl | rfl)
  · exact D.y_mem_range_partner 0
  · exact D.y_mem_range_partner 1

/-- **Finite presentation, derived.** -/
theorem finitelyPresented : Group.IsFinitelyPresented D.Routed :=
  routerQuotient_finitelyPresented D.relators_finite

/-- The protected ball condition, in the pairwise-difference shape the
free-group consumption point asks for.  The protected set is `{1, W-image of
the protected word}`, whose only nontrivial differences are that word and its
inverse. -/
theorem protectedBall_short : ∀ r ∈ symmetrization D.relators,
    ∀ x ∈ ({1, FreeGroup.lift D.W D.protectedWord} : Set RouterFree),
    ∀ z ∈ ({1, FreeGroup.lift D.W D.protectedWord} : Set RouterFree),
    2 * FreeGroup.norm (x / z) ≤ r.length := by
  intro r hr x hx z hz
  simp only [Set.mem_insert_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hx hz
  have hball := D.protectedBall r hr
  rcases hx with rfl | rfl <;> rcases hz with rfl | rfl
  · rw [div_self', FreeGroup.norm_one]
    omega
  · rw [one_div, FreeGroup.norm_inv_eq]
    exact hball
  · rw [div_one]
    exact hball
  · rw [div_self', FreeGroup.norm_one]
    omega

/-- **The word-combinatorial input, as a hypothesis.**  Injectivity of the
presentation map on the protected pair, and torsion-freeness of the routed
quotient.

This is verbatim the conclusion of
`GreendlingerFreeGate.router_conclusions_of_metric` at this design's data, and
the design's own fields are verbatim its hypothesis list, so the two fit with
no adapter — `Sofic.BespokeRouterGateConsumption` does the fitting.  It is a
hypothesis here rather than a call there because this module is root-imported
and the gate file still has open leaves; importing it would put those leaves
into the root's transitive closure and redden the shared build. -/
def RouterConclusions : Prop :=
  Set.InjOn (mkR D.relators)
      ({1, FreeGroup.lift D.W D.protectedWord} : Set RouterFree) ∧
    IsPowerTorsionFree D.Routed

/-- **Torsion-freeness**, the second half of the hypothesis. -/
theorem torsionFree (h : D.RouterConclusions) : IsPowerTorsionFree D.Routed :=
  h.2

/-- **Protected survival**, the first half of the hypothesis.  The
protected-set engine is used genuinely rather than restated: the ball condition
is its shortness hypothesis, and its conclusion is that the protected target
word survives, which is nontrivial because the design makes that word
nontrivial in the free group. -/
theorem protected_injOn (h : D.RouterConclusions) :
    Set.InjOn D.route ({1, s} : Set E) := by
  refine injOn_pair fun h1 => ?_
  exfalso
  refine D.protectedWord_ne_one ?_
  have hw : mkR D.relators (FreeGroup.lift D.W D.protectedWord)
      = mkR D.relators 1 := by
    rw [map_one, ← D.route_srcHom D.protectedWord, D.protectedWord_spec]
    exact h1
  exact h.1 (Set.mem_insert_of_mem _ rfl) (Set.mem_insert _ _) hw

/-- **The router's output.**  Every field of the frozen interface: all the
structural ones from the design alone, the two word-combinatorial ones from the
single hypothesis. -/
noncomputable def routerData [N.Normal] (h : D.RouterConclusions) :
    RoutingLemmaData E N s B where
  Quotient := D.Routed
  groupQuotient := inferInstance
  route := D.route
  route_surjective := D.route_surjective
  partner := D.partner
  partner_surjective := D.partner_surjective
  finitelyPresented := D.finitelyPresented
  defect_top := D.defect_top
  protected_injOn := D.protected_injOn h
  generatorOne := D.genOne
  generatorTwo := D.genTwo
  generates := D.generates
  torsionFree := D.torsionFree h

end RouterRelatorDesign

end BespokeRouter
end GroupApproximation
